{"causes":["The increasing complexity of industrial processes in the early 20th century necessitated structured management approaches.","Post-World War II industrial recovery in Japan created demand for effective quality management solutions.","The rise of statistical quality control methods laid the groundwork for iterative management techniques like PDCA."],"effects":["The PDCA cycle has become foundational in modern quality management systems, influencing methodologies like TQM and Six Sigma.","Organizations globally have adopted the PDCA cycle to enhance efficiency and product quality, leading to significant economic growth.","The principles of continuous improvement established by the PDCA cycle have permeated various sectors, including healthcare and education."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"w-edwards-deming","sourceName":"W. Edwards Deming","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"plan-do-check-adjust","targetName":"Plan, Do, Check, Adjust","context":"W. Edwards Deming's principles laid the groundwork for the PDCA cycle, which became essential in quality management."},{"sourceSlug":"total-quality-management","sourceName":"Total Quality Management","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"plan-do-check-adjust","targetName":"Plan, Do, Check, Adjust","context":"Total Quality Management incorporates the PDCA cycle as a core component for continuous improvement."},{"sourceSlug":"japan-post-wwii","sourceName":"Japan Post World War II","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"plan-do-check-adjust","targetName":"Plan, Do, Check, Adjust","context":"The PDCA cycle gained prominence in Japan's industrial recovery during the 1950s."},{"sourceSlug":"six-sigma","sourceName":"Six Sigma","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"plan-do-check-adjust","targetName":"Plan, Do, Check, Adjust","context":"Six Sigma methodologies utilize the PDCA cycle for process improvement and quality enhancement."},{"sourceSlug":"quality-management-systems","sourceName":"Quality Management Systems","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"plan-do-check-adjust","targetName":"Plan, Do, Check, Adjust","context":"The PDCA cycle is a fundamental element of modern Quality Management Systems utilized globally."}],"places":[{"name":"Tokyo, Japan","role":"Major development site"},{"name":"Detroit, USA","role":"Industrial hub of influence"},{"name":"Los Angeles, USA","role":"Service industry adoption"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: plan, do, check, adjust","type":"Reference article","slug":"plan-do-check-adjust"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q820214","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PDCA"],"tags":["methodology","Political & Polemical Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Plan-Do-Check-Act (PDCA, also known as the Deming cycle, Shewhart cycle, or PDSA — Plan-Do-Study-Act in W. Edwards Deming's preferred formulation) is a four-stage iterative management methodology for continuous improvement of processes, products, and services — one of the foundational frameworks of quality management, lean manufacturing, and organisational improvement. The cycle consists of four stages: Plan (identify an opportunity for improvement and plan a change), Do (implement the change on a small scale), Check/Study (analyse the results and determine whether the change achieved the desired improvement), and Act/Adjust (if the improvement was successful, implement it on a larger scale; if not, revise the plan and repeat the cycle). The methodology is designed to be iterative — the 'Act' stage feeds back into the 'Plan' stage of the next cycle — creating a continuous spiral of learning and improvement.

The PDCA cycle was developed by Walter A. Shewhart (1891–1967) — the American physicist and statistician who pioneered statistical process control at Bell Telephone Laboratories — and was popularised and elaborated by W. Edwards Deming (1900–1993), who introduced it to Japanese industry during the post-war reconstruction period (from 1950 onwards). Deming's influence on Japanese manufacturing quality — through his lectures to Japanese engineers and managers (the Union of Japanese Scientists and Engineers, JUSE, from 1950) and his championing of statistical methods and continuous improvement — is credited as a major factor in Japan's post-war industrial transformation and the development of the Toyota Production System, Total Quality Management (TQM), and the just-in-time manufacturing revolution. The Deming Prize (established by JUSE in 1950) is Japan's most prestigious quality management award, named in Deming's honour.

In the late 20th and early 21st centuries, PDCA was incorporated into the ISO 9001 quality management standard, the Six Sigma methodology, the ISO 14001 environmental management standard, and numerous other management frameworks, making it one of the most widely applied management methodologies in the world — used in manufacturing, healthcare, education, software development, and public administration.","subjects":["Management Science","Modern Era","Quality Management","Deming","Continuous Improvement","Manufacturing","Methodology","Business"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The PDCA cycle revolutionized management practices, contributing to the success of global quality movements that have economically transformed industries, particularly in Japan and the United States.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
